      "MRO": -74,
      "Mars Odyssey": -53,
      "ExoMars TGO": -143,
      "Mars Express": -41
    },
    "data_availability": "All four spacecraft returned all 1480 samples. Horizons did not offer an IAU_MARS body-fixed frame for vectors, so the inertial ICRF vectors were rotated into the Mars body-fixed frame by this script.",
    "frame": "ICRF -> Mars body-fixed with the IAU 2015 Mars pole and prime meridian (alpha0, delta0, W with their periodic terms), matrix Rz(W) Rx(90deg - delta0) Rz(90deg + alpha0). Pole direction checked at J2000. Time for the rotation: TDB = Horizons UT + 69.184 s (the constant offset Horizons itself uses for this date).",
    "observer": "East longitude +137.381, latitude -4.808, positive east, so Horizons' observer-table longitude sign convention never enters. Headline variant A: sphere of 3,396,190 m, zero height, latitude taken as planetocentric. Follow-up variants B to E are listed under summary.variants.",
    "angles": "Local east-north-up frame at the site. Azimuth clockwise from north, elevation above the local horizontal plane, geometric (no refraction, no light-time correction in variants A to D; variant E adds Horizons' light-time correction).",
    "pass_definition": "A pass is a continuous run of one-minute samples with elevation > 0 deg. Rise and set are the first and last whole minute of the run; interpolated values are linear zero crossings. Minute 0 is 2025-12-18 00:00 UTC. Old and new passes were paired by overlapping minute ranges; none was left unpaired and none touches the window edges.",
    "not_tuned": "Variant A was fixed before any comparison was run. Variants B to E and the height fit were added after seeing A disagree, only to find the cause; they are not an independent confirmation by themselves.",
    "first_computation_observer": "tools/comms_fetch.py asks Horizons for a geodetic observer at east longitude 137.381, latitude -4.808, height -3.6 km (SITE = \"-137.381,-4.808,-3.6\", COORD_TYPE GEODETIC). Variant D below (ellipsoid, -4.0 km) is the closest to it; the height fit gives -3.6 km."
  },
